Subject: [PATCH] ufs: qcom: resolve clock OPPs through operating-points-v2

required-opps points to dependent OPP entries such as a power-domain level,
not this controller's clock OPP table. Reading it as the table ignores
Spacewar's 75/150/300 MHz clock entries and selects the 150 MHz fallback.
Use operating-points-v2, preserving the legacy freq-table-hz fallback.

Primary binding: Linux v6.12 Documentation/devicetree/bindings/ufs/ufs-common.yaml
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/ufs/ufs-common.yaml#L23-L38
It defines operating-points-v2 as the clock OPP table whose opp-hz values
follow clock-names order. This patch fixes lookup only, without assuming
that the clock provider has programmed the maximum hardware rate.

diff --git a/drivers/ufs/ufs-qcom.c b/drivers/ufs/ufs-qcom.c
--- a/drivers/ufs/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -326,7 +326,7 @@
 		goto fallback;
 
 	/* Try parsing the opps */
-	if (!ofnode_parse_phandle_with_args(node, "required-opps",
+	if (!ofnode_parse_phandle_with_args(node, "operating-points-v2",
 					    NULL, 0, 0, &opp_table) &&
 	    ofnode_device_is_compatible(opp_table.node, "operating-points-v2")) {
 		ofnode opp_node;
